Q: What is the struct tag of anonymous structs in C? A: <p>it's not specified, but you can assume that is some random name that you don't have access to.  This makes every anonymous <code>struct</code> impossible to reuse (except if you have aliased it with a <code>typedef</code> or, as you do, you use the <code>typeof</code> operator) and different to any other.  It would be as if you have declared</p>
<pre><code>struct aspdoijaf {
    int x;
    double y;
} __my_var1__;

struct apiojahhih {
    int x;
    double y;
} __my_var2__;
</code></pre>
<p>and both <code>struct aspdoijaf</code> is a different type as <code>struct apiojahhih</code>.  The fact of being declred exactly equal internally is irrelevant, both types are different, so the variables are incompatible.  This means they cannot be assigned directly one to another, or passed as parameters to functions, or returned as values from them.</p>
